[{"experiment_id": "sme_fp32_tiles", "recommendation": "Experiment: reuse one packed A row panel across adjacent output-column tiles. Keep the existing four FP32 ZA selectors mapped to a 2VL\u00d72VL output tile, and process one row panel across all j tiles before moving to the next panel. For each reduction step, load the two A vectors once and use them for selectors 0 and 1 with the first B vector, then selectors 2 and 3 with the second B vector; load the two B vectors for each column tile. This is distinct from the supplied panel-pack experiments: those repack A through ZA, while this tests whether the existing full transpose can be scheduled to reuse A across j tiles without changing its layout. Hypothesis: fewer repeated A loads may improve input reuse; the larger live/reuse span may instead reduce cache efficiency. Keep increasing-k accumulation order and the existing predicated tail handling. Checks: verify only selectors 0\u20133 are used, all output elements are written for odd and partial tiles, n<=0 returns, and public correctness stays within 0.002. Then measure serially against fresh AC controls under the fixed benchmark; treat any score change as unverified until repeated paired measurements establish it.", "risks": "A vectors may already stay in cache, so reduced loads may not help; the altered loop order may worsen B locality or increase address and loop overhead. Do not infer a gain from historical battery scores or assign any joint outcome to this advice alone."}, {"experiment_id": "sme_fp32_schedule", "recommendation": "Experiment: test a two-accumulator schedule using ZA selectors 0 and 1 for two adjacent column tiles of one row tile. For each k, load one A vector and two B vectors, then issue the two FP32 MOPA updates in increasing k order. Store those two tiles before moving to the next row tile. Keep the existing transpose, four FP32 MOPA selector limit, general tail path, and all fixed benchmark rules. This changes the schedule by replacing the 2\u00d72 row-and-column tile with a 1\u00d72 tile; it tests whether fewer live input vectors and a shorter accumulator dependency path improve the loop. It is a proposal only, with no measured evidence of benefit.", "risks": "The smaller output tile may double row-tile loop work and reduce arithmetic per loaded B value. It may also increase total B traffic. Confirm all four ZA selectors are not needed, handle odd dimensions and incomplete tiles without out-of-bounds loads or stores, check the fixed 0.002 error limit, and compare serially with fresh AC controls. Do not infer a gain from historical noisy scores or assign any joint result to this proposal alone."}, {"experiment_id": "register_reuse", "recommendation": "Experiment: keep the existing four ZA accumulators and 2VL\u00d72VL output tile, but unroll the reduction loop by two while reusing each loaded A vector across the two adjacent B column vectors for both k steps. For each k, load A0/A1 once and B0/B1 once, then issue the four MOPA selectors; repeat for k+1. This tests whether explicit input reuse and a small reduction unroll improve scheduling while preserving increasing-k FP32 accumulation. Keep the current tail path unchanged and use the general path for sizes that do not have full tiles.", "risks": "This overlaps the historical two-step load pipeline experiment, whose scores did not establish a gain. The proposed version should differ by reusing each A vector across both column updates before loading the next reduction step, rather than keeping eight vectors live together. Added live vectors or scheduling constraints may still raise register pressure or provide no benefit. Do not infer a gain from prior battery scores."}, {"experiment_id": "a_transpose", "recommendation": "Experiment: fuse A transposition with consumption, so each row-major A tile is loaded into ZA once and its transposed vectors feed the four FP32 MOPA accumulators directly. Keep the existing 2VL\u00d72VL output tile and increasing-k accumulation order; do not write a full transposed matrix or packed copy. This removes the separate full-matrix transpose and its scratch allocation from the timed path. For tails, use predicates and in-bounds inactive-vector addresses as in the general path. Hypothesis: avoiding the full transpose write and reread reduces total memory traffic and call cost across n. This is a distinct A-layout experiment; it changes the transpose/consumption boundary rather than packing or MOPA scheduling.", "risks": "ZA transpose instructions may add enough work or constrain scheduling to outweigh the removed memory traffic. Loading A tiles again for each output-column tile may increase A traffic substantially, especially for large n. Small n may not amortize setup. Validate all supported n, including odd tails and n<=0, against the fixed 0.002 error limit. Measure complete gemm time, including allocation if any, transpose work, and consumption, with fresh serial AC controls and repeated trials; compare the spread without inferring a gain from noisy scores. Keep FP32 arithmetic, ABI, thread count, selector range, and benchmark fixed."}, {"experiment_id": "panel_packing", "recommendation": "Experiment: pack each B panel of 2VL adjacent columns into a compact, k-major scratch layout inside gemm, with each reduction row storing those columns contiguously. For each output row panel, reuse the packed B panel while computing its rows; keep the existing FP32 SME outer products, accumulation order, and selectors 0\u20133. Allocate scratch in gemm, include packing and allocation in the timed call, and free it before return. Hypothesis: compact B panel data may improve cache reuse and reduce repeated row-stride address work across the output-row panels. Handle the final partial column panel with predicated loads/stores; use checked size arithmetic, preserve n<=0 behavior, and provide the existing scalar fallback if allocation fails. This is distinct from prior A-row-panel packing. Verify