\u00d72VL SME output tile with column-major ZA stores to a temporary contiguous C tile, then copy each tile row to row-major C with contiguous scalar/vector stores. Keep the existing full A transpose, FP32 MOPA selectors 0\u20133, reduction order, tile coverage, and general predicated tail path. This isolates whether ZA vertical stores plus a row copy improve output-store efficiency; all scratch allocation and copying stay inside gemm. Do not assume alignment: malloc aligns the scratch base, but tile row starts are separated by the active tile width and may not meet any stronger alignment requirement. Use unaligned-safe stores unless each address is proven aligned.", "risks": "The extra copy and scratch traffic may cost more than the existing direct horizontal ZA stores. The change must preserve every output exactly within the fixed 0.002 relative max-error limit, including odd tails and n<=0. It is a hypothesis only; assess it with the unchanged correctness checks and fresh serial AC controls. The supplied history contains no evidence that this store layout is faster."}, {"experiment_id": "loop_order", "recommendation": "Propose a loop-order experiment that transposes B inside gemm, then computes output in row-major order with loops `i`, `j` in small contiguous blocks, and `k` innermost: `sum += A[i*n+k] * Bt[j*n+k]`. This keeps each reduction sequential, accesses both reduction inputs contiguously, and writes C contiguously. Compare against the current SME variants to learn whether simple cache-friendly scalar/vectorizable loop ordering can offset the loss of SME outer products. Keep full FP32 arithmetic and handle all n tails; allocate and free Bt inside gemm. The supplied evidence does not establish this will be faster.", "risks": "This is an unmeasured design proposal. It may be much slower than SME MOPA because it gives up matrix outer-product instructions; transposing B adds timed work and allocation. Compiler reassociation under the fixed fast-math flags can also affect error, so check every output against the fixed 0.002 error limit. Verify n<=0, odd dimensions, and general sizes, and confirm the declared ABI and full k reduction are preserved."}, {"experiment_id": "address_generation", "recommendation": "Propose one experiment: in the general SME multiply path, replace per-k index expressions with advancing row pointers. At the start of each output tile, set `ap0 = at + i`, `ap1 = at + i + second_row`, `bp0 = b + j`, and `bp1 = b + j + second_col`. Each reduction step loads from those pointers, then advances them by `n` only when another k step remains. Keep the existing predicates and ZA selectors, and retain the current tile and tail handling. This isolates address-generation overhead while keeping the FP32 operations and reduction order unchanged.", "risks": "The compiler may already form equivalent pointer increments, so the change may have no effect. Conditional final-step increments add a branch; unconditional increments risk forming pointers beyond the valid allocation at the final step. Verify no out-of-bounds pointer formation for odd dimensions and partial tiles, including n=1 and n<=0; check general correctness against the fixed 0.002 limit and then measure serially against fresh AC controls. Treat any score as unverified until those measurements; do not infer causality from noisy or joint results."}, {"experiment_id": "compiler_codegen", "recommendation": "Propose an experiment that removes repeated index multiplication from the full-tile n=512 SME reduction loop. In the n=512 fast path, initialize row-panel pointers to `at + i` and column-panel pointers to `B + j`; after each k step, advance both by 512 floats. Keep the same four ZA tiles, FP32 MOPA selectors 0\u20133, increasing-k update order, and a separately checked final step or bounded loop so no pointer advances beyond the valid matrix. This tests whether Apple clang 21 emits cheaper pointer increments than repeated `k * 512` address calculations. Keep the existing general path for other sizes and tails. Hypothesis only; supplied evidence contains no compiler output or measurements for this change.", "risks": "The compiler may already strength-reduce the constant-stride indexing, so generated code and performance may be unchanged. Pointer updates can also increase dependency-chain cost. Check the compiled assembly for the reduction loop, then verify public correctness for n=512 and general sizes including odd tails and n<=0 under the fixed 0.002 error limit. Measure serially against fresh AC controls before making any performance claim. No supplied evidence verifies this proposal."}, {"experiment_id": "tail_alignment", "recommendation": "Experiment: add a dedicated output-tile path for complete 2VL-by-2VL tiles, while retaining the existing predicated path for every partial tile. In the full-tile path, use all-active predicates and form each load address from the tile base plus a reduction offset; keep the existing FP32 MOPA selectors 0\u20133 and increasing-k update order. This tests whether removing tail predicates and their offset handling from the common full-tile work improves execution, without assuming that n is divisible by the vector width. Check n<=0, n smaller than VL, odd n, and sizes just below and above VL and 2VL boundaries. Confirm each full-tile load and store lies within its matrix and each partial tile uses predicates and in-bounds fallback addresses for inactive vectors. Check the fixed error limit 0.002 across required shapes. This is advice only; it has not been compiled, tested, or measured.", "risks": "This overlaps with the prior full-tile specialization advice, so it may not count as a sufficiently distinct experiment. The historical results show large variance and no established gain. The compiler may already remove equivalent predicate work. Tail-path address or predicate mistakes could cause out-of-bounds access or incorrect results."}, {"experiment_id": "n512_fast_path", "recommendation": "Experiment: add a guarded n=512 multiplication path that processes two consecutive k values per iteration. Load both steps' A and B vectors, then apply the four ZA MOPA selectors for k and k+1 in increasing order. Keep the existing full transpose and general predicated path. Enter the fast path only when n==512 and 512 is divisible by 2*svcntsw(), so each tile is full. Hypothesis: fewer loop branches and overlapped vector loads may reduce reduction-loop overhead. This is distinct from the prior one-step constant-bound path and two-step preloaded pipeline: it specializes the n=512 loop and interleaves loads with ordered updates. Advice only; performance is unverified.", "risks": "Extra live vectors may increase register pressure or cause spills; the compiler may already schedule loads well. Verify compilation with the fixed clang flags, error <=0.002 at n=512, and correctness on odd sizes, sizes that do not tile the streaming vector length, and n<=0 through the general path. Confirm only selectors 0..3 are used and scratch allocation remains inside gemm. Measure serially against fresh AC controls with the frozen workload and repeated-control rules; do not infer a gain from historical battery scores."}, {"experiment_id": "b_panel_layout", "recommendation": "Experiment: pack each 2VL-wide column panel of B into contiguous k-major storage, with each reduction row containing the panel's columns. Reuse that packed panel across row tiles. Keep the existing four ZA accumulators and increasing-k FP32 MOPA updates; do not change the transpose of A or the general gemm ABI. Allocate and fill the panel buffer inside gemm so setup cost stays in the timed call. Hypothesis: contiguous, compact B loads may reduce address and cache costs in the SME consumer enough to offset packing. This is unverified advice, not a measured fact.", "risks": "Packing adds a full read and write of B for each panel and may cost more than it saves. A packed panel may also displace A or output